WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:05.070
In the last video, we talked about how the
Fourier series can be used to estimate a lot

00:00:05.070 --> 00:00:10.049
of weird functions on an interval by just
using functions of this form:

00:00:10.049 --> 00:00:17.010
Which we called the complex exponentials.
Remember if we wanted to estimate some function,

00:00:17.010 --> 00:00:23.600
we multiplying each of the exponentials functions
by a number, called its weight, and then added

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:31.950
the functions together. We found that for
a lot of functions, this worked really well.

00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:36.660
The next natural question is whether we can
use the same trick to estimate a function

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:42.790
on the whole real line. It's tempting to say,
if a certain combination estimates the function

00:00:42.790 --> 00:00:47.620
well on the interval, surely the same combination
must estimate the function well on the whole

00:00:47.620 --> 00:00:53.620
line. That's not the case at all though, as
you can see from these examples.

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:58.149
That's where the Fourier Transform comes in.
It comes up with a way to estimate a function

00:00:58.149 --> 00:01:04.960
on the whole real line using complex exponentials.
Before we talk about how, let's think about

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:11.020
the complex function e to the power of I k
x. The real part of this is this cos function

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:16.680
and the imaginary part is a sin function and
both bits have the same wavelength. How does

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:23.159
that wavelength relate to k? Well k is in
fact 2 pi divided by the wavelength. We call

00:01:23.159 --> 00:01:29.440
k the wave number. If we think back to the
fourier series, we only needed to use exponentials

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:34.890
with certain values of k. Unfortunately, that's
not the case if we want our estimates to work

00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:41.010
for the whole line. We need to consider the
exponentials with all possible k values. Now

00:01:41.010 --> 00:01:46.920
say we have a function f that we want to approximate
on the real line. We have to figure out exactly

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:53.299
how much of each one of these exponentials
we need to add to get a good approximation.

00:01:53.299 --> 00:01:58.380
So in other words, we're looking for some
function of k, something that takes a value

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:03.670
of k and tells you how much of the corresponding
exponential you need to add. This is pretty

00:02:03.670 --> 00:02:09.679
much what the Fourier transform is. Let f
hat be the fourier transform of a function.

00:02:09.679 --> 00:02:13.709
Then this equation happens.

00:02:13.709 --> 00:02:19.819
Ok, don't freak out. I know there's an integral
there, and so it seems really hard to understand

00:02:19.819 --> 00:02:24.090
what's actually going on but -its really not.
If you've seen my calculus video, or you know

00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:30.140
something about calculus already, you know
that the integral itself is about an approximation,

00:02:30.140 --> 00:02:34.230
so we'll come at this from that angle.

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:39.170
First we pick a small number, let's start
with half. What we're going to do is get the

00:02:39.170 --> 00:02:44.401
best approximation we can to our function
using only the complex exponentials with k

00:02:44.401 --> 00:02:50.230
values that are multiples of half. Our task
is to find the weights to put in front of

00:02:50.230 --> 00:02:57.030
these functions. OK, suppose we know the fourier
transform of our function already. We're going

00:02:57.030 --> 00:03:02.511
to restrict our attention to the points on
the graph where k is a multiple of half. It's

00:03:02.511 --> 00:03:06.770
tempting to think the weights to put in front
of each of the exponentials should just be

00:03:06.770 --> 00:03:13.090
given by the value of the fourier transform
at that k, but that's not quite right. Actually,

00:03:13.090 --> 00:03:17.801
the appropriate weight is the area of each
of these sections, in other words, the fourier

00:03:17.801 --> 00:03:23.640
transform multiplied by half. Let's see just
how good or bad this approximation is to our

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:31.651
actual function. Not awful actually. The right
shape at least. But we can do better. Let's

00:03:31.651 --> 00:03:37.519
try using delta x equals one quarter.
Now, we've got more exponentials to work with.

00:03:37.519 --> 00:03:42.371
We'll use a similar process to find the weights.
We'll look at the relevant parts of the fourier

00:03:42.371 --> 00:03:48.190
transform. Again the weights are given by
these areas, which in this case is the height

00:03:48.190 --> 00:03:54.549
multiplied by one quarter. Now let's see how
good this is. A bit better than last time.

00:03:54.549 --> 00:04:03.709
So we can go ahead and do the same process
with delta x equaling a one tenth and then

00:04:03.709 --> 00:04:07.870
by the time delta x equals one one hundredth,
you can hardly see the difference between

00:04:07.870 --> 00:04:12.150
the two functions.
If we wanted to summaries what we just did

00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:14.690
in equation form, we'd say that our function
approximately equals the sum of f hat of k

00:04:14.690 --> 00:04:18.930
times dx times e to the power of (ikx), where
each k a multiple of delta x. The approximation

00:04:18.930 --> 00:04:22.850
gets better, the smaller we make k, so we
can say that f equals the limit of delta x

00:04:22.850 --> 00:04:28.680
goes to 0 of all that stuff on the right hand
side. But this is, by definition, is the integral

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:35.159
of f hat k times e to the ikx dx. This, if
you remember, is the integral we were trying

00:04:35.159 --> 00:04:41.190
to explain. So you can see that the fourier
transform function tells us which of the exponential

00:04:41.190 --> 00:04:45.249
functions to give the most and the least weight
to when we approximate a function.

00:04:45.249 --> 00:04:49.409
With that in mind, it's hard to imagine what
the relationship between a function and a

00:04:49.409 --> 00:04:54.599
fourier transform is. The surprising fact
is though that there is some interesting links

00:04:54.599 --> 00:05:00.169
between the two. Next episode, we'll explore
this relationship. In particular, I'll explain

00:05:00.169 --> 00:05:06.349
why the fourier transform makes the Heisenberg
uncertainty principle happen.

